>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `looper_ss`

``` hol4
simpLib.looper_ss : string * (simpset -> tactic) -> ssfrag
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Packages one named tactic looper as a simpset fragment.

A call to `looper_ss (name, make_tac)` creates an anonymous `ssfrag` that
contains the looper and no rewrite rules, conversions, congruences,
decision procedures, or solvers.  It can be merged into a simpset with
`++`, or combined with other fragments using `merge_ss`.

When a fragment is merged, looper registration follows the same rules as
`add_looper`.  A new name is appended after existing loopers.  An
existing name is updated in place, retaining its priority.  Multiple
loopers in merged fragments are processed in fragment-list order.  At
run time the first applicable registered looper is selected.

The function `make_tac` is passed the active, per-invocation simpset.  Its
tactic is tried only after ordinary rewriting and final solvers fail.
If it succeeds, the complete simplifier sequence is restarted on each
subgoal.  It must fail when inapplicable and must make progress whenever
it succeeds; `Conv.UNCHANGED` is interpreted as failure.

The simpset bound installed by `simpLib.limit n` permits at most `n`
successful looper rounds in one top-level simplification-tactic call.
The counter is shared by all subgoals.  With no bound, a looper that
succeeds without progress may diverge.

The looper registration name is independent of the fragment name.  The
result of `looper_ss` is anonymous; apply `name_ss` if the fragment must
be removable with `remove_ssfrags` or suppressible with `ExclSF`.
Regardless of the fragment name, `Excl name` suppresses this particular
looper for one theorem-list invocation.

Looper payloads are tactic hooks.  They are used by simplification
tactics based on `GEN_SIMP_TAC`, but ignored by the conversion and rule
interfaces `SIMP_CONV`, `SIMP_PROVE`, `SIMP_RULE`, and `ASM_SIMP_RULE`.

### Failure

`looper_ss` never fails and does not run the supplied function.  Giving
the resulting anonymous fragment to an operation that requires a named
fragment can fail unless it has first been passed through `name_ss`.

### Example

``` hol4
val CONJ_LOOP_ss =
  simpLib.looper_ss
    ("split conjunctions", fn _ => CONJ_TAC)
  |> simpLib.name_ss "CONJ_LOOP"

val ss = simpLib.empty_ss ++ CONJ_LOOP_ss
```

Here `Excl "split conjunctions"` disables the tactic while retaining the
fragment.  `ExclSF "CONJ_LOOP"` removes the named fragment, including
its looper, for that invocation.

